            <title xml:id="title_id3ee13ec8" xml:lang="de">Capriccio für die Violine mit Orchester oder Clavierbegleitung</title>
            <title xml:id="title_9f5bcfb4" xml:lang="da">Capriccio for violin med orkester eller klaver</title>
            <title xml:id="title_4055d95e" xml:lang="en">Capriccio for Violin with Orchestra or Piano</title>
            <contributor xml:id="respStmt_5d1ce27b">
               <persName role="composer" xml:id="persName_id7850a996">Niels W. Gade</persName>
               <persName role="dedicatee" xml:id="persName_592fd962">Johann Lauterbach</persName>
            </contributor>
            <creation xml:id="creation_idbbafa7f8">
               <date isodate="1878-05" xml:id="date_79b23251">May 1878</date>
               <geogName xml:id="geogName_idfb4a8b59">Copenhagen</geogName>
            </creation>
            <history xml:id="history_id7b87e79a">
               <p xml:id="idm113">The Capriccio was written at the instigation of the German violinist Johann Lauterbach in Dresden, who wanted a short piece for violin and orchestra to be performed in connection with the festivities on the occasion of the silver wedding of the Saxonian King Albert and his wife Carola in June 1879. Gade sent Lauterbach a version for violin and piano, asking him to orchestrate it himself according to Gade's guidelines. Gade emphasized that it was for Lauterbach's use only. Since the autograph apparently has not survived, it is not known how close the posthumous editions by Franz Ries and Carl Reinecke are to Gade's original, but it is more than likely that they used the same source.</p>
            </history>
